[08:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Mistake four, the yes machine. Claude code asks permission before it runs commands or edits files. Beginners handle this in one of two'

[08:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'terrible ways. Way one, they sit there slamming allow allow allow 200 times a day until the brain switches off and they approve something they really'

[08:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'needed to read. Way two, they get sick of way one, find the flag called dangerously skip permissions and turn every safety off on day three. The docs are blunt about that flag.'

[08:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'It offers no protection against prompt injection or unintended actions. And it belongs in isolated environments like containers, not on your laptop next to your dot env files. And this is not hypothetical.'

[08:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'In December, a post hit the Claude AI subreddit and collected almost 2,000 upvotes. Quote, Claude CLI deleted my entire home directory, wiped my whole Mac.'

[09:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The user asked Claude to clean up packages in an old repo. The cleanup command Claude generated ended with a slash after a tilde. One character.'

[09:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'That is the entire home directory. Desktop, documents, keychain gone. Docker later wrote the whole thing up as a case study in coding agent horror stories. Now, the zero.'

[09:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Claude code lets you write three permission lists: allow, ask, and deny. Safe commands run instantly. Scary commands always ask. And deny rules block things outright.'

[09:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Like reading your secrets file. And a deny beats everything else. Out of 100 real setups, the number with a deny list was zero. Not few. Zero. 98 had no permissions file at all.'

[10:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The fix takes 10 minutes. Allow your everyday commands so the prompts stop wearing you down. Then write three deny rules: your ENV files,'

[10:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'force pushes, production deploys. You kill the fatigue and you put a hard floor under the worst today. Mistake five, the hoarder. This one is sponsored by every listicle on the internet.'

[10:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '10 MCP servers you need. 15 tools that will change your life. And beginners install all of them. Wire Claude into browsers, databases, design tools, note apps, and then wonder'

[10:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'why everything feels slow and cluttered. Let me give you real numbers instead of vibes. One developer measured his setup with everything switched on and found the tool definitions alone'

[10:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'were eating 82,000 tokens. 41% of his entire context window gone before he typed a single word. Now, fair is fair.'

[11:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '2026 fixed part of this. Tool definitions load on demand now. And one documented setup dropped from 50, 1,000 tokens down to 8 and 1/2 thousand.'

[11:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'But three costs never went away. Every extra tool is one more thing Claude has to choose between. One more thing that can break. And one more door into your machine. I ran this exact experiment on this'

[11:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'channel recently. Installed the 15 most recommended MCP servers and gave each one a real task. Nine of the 15 got uninstalled the same day.'

[11:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Dead repos, paywalled keys, tools that duplicate what Claude code already does. Even the official cost guidance says, "Prefer command-line tools when they exist, because they are cheaper than'

[11:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'MCP." So, the fix is a rule, not a list. Start from zero. Add a server only when a real task demands it. And once a month,'

[12:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'run /mcp and uninstall anything you have not used in 2 weeks. Your setup is not a Pokédex. You do not have to catch them all. Mistake six,'

[12:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the eternal session. This is the one quietly burning the most money. Beginners open one conversation on Monday and just live in it. Fix the login bug.'

[12:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Now, add a feature. Now, write my emails. Same session. Day four, 300 messages deep. Two problems. First,'

[12:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'cost and focus. Everything in that conversation rides along with every new request. Old errors, dead ends, that random question about dinner. You pay for all of it, every message,'

[12:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and the clutter actively makes the answers worse. Second, the cliff. When the window fills up, Claude code automatically compresses your history into a summary. And summaries lose detail. One of the most upvoted Claude code'

[13:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'posts ever written, over 2,300 upvotes, describes hitting that compression limit mid-feature and watching Claude lose the thread, recreate files it had already built, and spiral.'

[13:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'People swear the model got dumber when really their 3 days of context just got zipped into a paragraph. The fix is two commands and one habit. /clear between unrelated tasks.'

[13:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Fresh start, and the docs literally tell you to use it frequently. /compact with instructions when you need the same session but lighter. You can tell it exactly what to keep.'

[13:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Like, focus on the API changes. And /context which shows you a live map of what you\'re doing. So, the invisible tax becomes visible.'

[13:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And do not fear the fresh start. Old conversations are still there under /resume. Clearing costs you nothing. Holding costs you every single message.'

[14:09] [scene:talk]
